\begin_layout Subsection
ooRexx
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:ooRexx"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The programming language Open Object Rexx (ooRexx) is an object-oriented
 scripting language that was developed by IBM for its operating system OS/2.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

It is an enhancement of the classic programming language Rexx and was initially
 developed for the operating system VM/SP, and later ported to the platforms
 MVS, OS/2 and PC-DOS.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

In October 2004, IBM gave the ooRexx source code for a couple of operating
 systems, including Microsoft Windows, Sun Solaris and Linux, to the Rexx
 Language Association (RexxLA), which released it under the Common Public
 License (CPL).
 The clear advantage of ooRexx is, that its syntax has large similarities
 to the English language and therefore it is very easy to learn and to understan
d.
 Besides its simplicity, it is a very mighty programming language and fully
 supports object oriented programming.
 Additionally, ooRexx offers full downward compatibility to the classic
 REXX language.
\shape default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "seminar001"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
BSF4ooRexx
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:BSF4ooRexx"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The 'Bean Scripting Framework for Open Object Rexx' (in short BSF4ooRexx)
 is a project that enables a developer to access ooRexx from Java and vice
 versa.
 What started as proof-of-concept-project in the context of a seminar at
 the University of Essen, Germany, in winter semester 2000/2001, rapidly
 developed into a highly interesting project with a large number of followers.
 In 2003, the 'Augsburg' version of BSF4ooRexx was released, which made
 it possible to load Java from ooRexx and to interact with Java objects.
 The access itself was quite complex at this time and constantly required
 the developer to explicitly define the Java data types when calling a Java
 method.
 Because ooRexx, and therefore also BSF4ooRexx, are built upon the principle
 of simplicity, this problem had to be solved.
 Only three years later, the 'Vienna' version was released which not only
 improved the access to Java drastically, but also implemented support for
 OpenOffice.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

BSF4ooRexx makes it, for example, possible to create graphical user interfaces
 (in short GUIs) or to automate Java applications from within ooRexx scripts.
 Especially the last point becomes very important in connection with the
 automation of OpenOffice and lets one sense the high value of this project.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\shape default

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "seminar001"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Installers
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Installers"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Installers are software programs that install applications on a user's operating
 system.
 Such installers may have a graphical interface and typically include procedures
 for installing and uninstalling software programs.
 Sophisticated installers also have a procedure for reinstalling, in order
 to add additional features afterwards or to repair a defective installation.
 Depending on their capabilities, a large number of installers can be distinguis
hed.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The simplest form of an installer only copies files from the installer package
 to a destination directory on the user's hard disk.
 In this case, no scripts of any kind are executed before or after copying.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

On the other hand, there are installers that can execute scripts before
 or after the copy process.
 Depending on the time of execution, such scripts are called preflight or
 postflight.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Additionally, installers can also check if specific preconditions are met.
 For example, if the required Java version for the program to be installed,
 is present.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The advantages and disadvantages of such installers will be discussed in
 further detail in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Advantages"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Disadvantages"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Advantages
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Advantages"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main advantage of an installer is its simplicity.
 The user can install a sophisticated software program by simply executing
 the installer.
 He or she does not have to worry about where files and folders have to
 be copied in order to make the program work.
 Most installers will also have an uninstall function, which allows the
 user to cleanly reverse all changes made during the installation without
 negatively influencing other installed applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Disadvantages
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Disadvantages"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary disadvantage is, that in most installers the users possibilities
 to intervene is limited.
 Although this may not be a problem for inexperienced users, it could be
 problematic for advanced users, such as developers, that want to change
 the behavior of the installer.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Due to their large number of features, the initial creation of such installers
 is quite complex, as it will be shown in the following chapters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Research Question
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on the advantages of installers the main goal of this thesis is to
 create graphical installer packages for Windows, Linux and Mac OS X.
 Such installers are easier to use than the existing installation methods
 which have to be executed from the operating system's command line.
 This fact is daunting for inexperienced users that normally don't work
 this way.
 To make it more attractive for such persons, as well as more experienced
 users, this thesis' research question is, which possibilities for creating
 an installer for a specific operating system exist, which the most appropriate
 one for the given requirements is, how an installer is created using the
 chosen technology and what the resulting benefits are.
\end_layout

