\begin_layout Section
DEB Package for Debian-based Linux Distributions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:DEB-Package"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Author: Jürgen Hesse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the creation of the installer package for Debian-based Linux distributions,
 we used the Debian Package Management System (
\family typewriter
dpkg
\family default
) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dpkg-homepage"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:The-DEB-Package-System"

\end_inset

 will take a closer look on the features of this system, followed by chapter
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Creating-the-DEB-Package"

\end_inset

, which shows how a package will be created.
 Last but not least, chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Use-Cases-DEB"

\end_inset

 will show the package in action.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The DEB Package System
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:The-DEB-Package-System"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
A Debian package, or a Debian archive file, contains the executable files,
 libraries, and documentation associated with a particular suite of program
 or set of related programs.
 Normally, a Debian archive file has a filename that ends in 
\family typewriter
.deb
\family default
 
\shape default

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "debian-faq-packages"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following chapters will show how a Debian package for BSF4ooRexx can
 be built.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Creating the DEB Package for Debian-based Linux Distributions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Creating-the-DEB-Package"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following chapters describe the required steps to create a Debian (DEB)
 package.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Install the required software
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Actually, there are no special requirements that have to be met to create
 packages on Debian-based Linux systems.
 It is not necessary to install additional software components, because
 the required components are already present in every standard installation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nevertheless, it is highly recommended to install a good text editor for
 editing the packages install scripts and the meta data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For installing the newly created package, on the other hand, it will be
 required to have the appropriate versions of Java and ooRexx installed.
 We will take a closer look at this issue in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Meta-data-DEB"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Root folder 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Root-folder-DEB"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The root folder contains the files that will be installed on the target
 system.
 While installation, the directory structure given in the root folder will
 be copied to the target file system and additional scripts will be executed.
 These additional scripts are contained in a folder called 
\family typewriter
DEBIAN
\family default
 within the root folder.
 To be executed correctly at the right time during installation, the additional
 scripts must follow explicit naming conventions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-package-basics"

\end_inset

.
 We will take a look at this issue in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Install-scripts-DEB"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The root folder must have the same name as the resulting package.
 Generally, this name should follow the definition given in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-maintainer-guide"

\end_inset

: 
\shape italic

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

You must choose the package name to consist only of lower case letters (
\family typewriter
a-z
\family default
), digits (
\family typewriter
0-9
\family default
), plus (
\family typewriter
+
\family default
) and minus (
\family typewriter
-
\family default
) signs, and periods (
\family typewriter
.
\family default
).
 It must be at least two characters long, must start with an alphanumeric
 character, and must not be the same as existing ones.
 It is a good idea to keep its length within 30 characters.
 ...
 If you need to invent a version string, use the 
\family typewriter
YYYYMMDD
\family default
 format such as 
\family typewriter
20110429
\family default
 as upstream
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Upstream
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is an often used term for the software components a package is built for,
 e.g.
 the BSF4ooRexx files which are normally distributed as .zip-Archive.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 version.
 This ensures that 
\family typewriter
dpkg
\family default
 interprets later versions correctly as upgrades.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In our case, the project name is, of course, 
\family typewriter
bsf4oorexx
\family default
.
 The version and revision numbers follow the values given in BSF4ooRexx's
 
\family typewriter
BSF.CLS
\family default
.
 At the moment of writing this document it was 
\family typewriter
406.20110822
\family default
.
 Therefore, the version numbers have been chosen accordingly and the root
 folder called 
\family typewriter
bsf4oorexx-406.20110822
\family default
 has been created.
 As mentioned above, the term 
\family typewriter
20110822 
\family default
indicates that the package's content was built on August 22nd, 2011 and
 allows to give the user additional version information.
 After adding the installation files and the installation information, the
 package will finally have the structure depicted in figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Debian-package-structure-DEB"

\end_inset

.
 The necessary steps are described in the following chapters.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename images/linux/deb/Root_folder.png
	scale 60

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Debian package structure.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Debian-package-structure-DEB"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After creating the basic package structure, the next step is to create the
 package's meta data, which controls its behaviour while installation etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Meta data 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Meta-data-DEB"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The package’s meta data (also called control information) is stored in a
 file called 
\family typewriter
control
\family default
 inside the 
\family typewriter
DEBIAN
\family default
 folder of the root directory 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-package-basics"

\end_inset

.
 Its content is shown in listing 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "The-control-file-DEB-1"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
spacing{1}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset include
LatexCommand lstinputlisting
filename "/Users/juergenhesse/Projekte/Linux/Debian/build/bsf4oorexx-406.20110822/DEBIAN/control"
lstparams "basicstyle={\\footnotesize\\ttfamily},breaklines=true,caption={The control file.},captionpos=b,extendedchars=true,keywords={Package, Version, Section, Priority, Homepage, Maintainer, Architecture, Description, Depends, Recommends},label={The-control-file-DEB-1},morestring={[b]\"},numbers=left,numberstyle={\\scriptsize},showstringspaces=false,tabsize=4"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
endspacing
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family typewriter
Package
\family default
 simply describes the package’s name as it is defined in the root folder’s
 name.
 Please note that the version numbers etc.
 are omitted in the package name.
 Because the version information cannot be omitted completely it gets it’s
 own tag called 
\family typewriter
Version
\family default
.
 The version information is the second part of the root folder’s name, to
 be exact, everything between the hyphens 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-maintainer-guide"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Software packages for Debian-based Linux distributions are organized in
 sections.
 These sections describe what kind of software the user is dealing with.
 For example, the section 
\family typewriter
admin
\family default
 contains programs for administering the system.
 
\family typewriter
net
\family default
, on the other hand, contains software for networking, e.g.
 email clients etc.
 Because ooRexx and BSF4ooRexx are programming languages we are using the
 same section 
\family typewriter
as ooRexx, namely 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Interpreted Computer Languages
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
\family default
 here.
 This is done to ensure that users will find the BSF4ooRexx package in the
 same place as the ooRexx package.
 Nevertheless it would also be possible to use the predefined keyword 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
devel
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 here.
 The tag 
\family typewriter
priority
\family default
 describes how important this package is for an average user.
 For new packages the value 
\family typewriter
optional
\family default
 is the best choice.
 
\family typewriter
Homepage
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
Maintainer
\family default
 point to the developers of this package and are set accordingly.
 The architecture in this case is 
\family typewriter
all
\family default
 and refers therefore to both bitnesses of 32 bit and 64 bit.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-maintainer-guide"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two very important settings are 
\family typewriter
Depends
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
Recommends
\family default
.
 
\family typewriter
Depends
\family default
 defines a list of other packages (and their versions) that have to be present
 before the actual package can be installed.
 In our case we defined, that Java (Version 1.6.0 or higher) and ooRexx (Version
 4.1.0 or higher) have to be installed at least 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-maintainer-guide"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The possible settings for 
\family typewriter
Recommends
\family default
 work quire similar.
 It doesn’t list required packages, but packages that should be installed.
 In other words it lists packages that are recommended, as the tag name
 indicates.
 If they are not present the correct functionality of the actual package
 is not harmed.
 In our case, we recommend to install OpenOffice because BSF4ooRexx is great
 for OpenOffice automation.
 If the user doesn’t need the OpenOffice integration, he or she is still
 able to install the package without the OpenOffice capabilities.
 Finally, the 
\family typewriter
Description
\family default
 tag can be used to give a short summary about the package’s contents 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deb-maintainer-guide"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

