\begin_layout Subsection
The RPM Package Management System
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:The-RPM-System"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
The RPM Package Manager (RPM) is a ...
 command line driven package management system capable of installing, uninstalli
ng, ...
 and updating computer software packages.
 Each software package consists of files along with information about the
 package like its version, a description, and the like
\shape default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rpm-homepage"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The basic functionality of RPM is very similar to the features of the Debian
 package management system, described in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:DEB-Package"

\end_inset

.
 RPM uses a bunch of source files to create a package, based on the information
 defined in a control- or, what RPM calls it, package specification (in
 short 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

spec
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) file.
 This file holds information like the package's base name (i.e.
 the name without any version information), the version, a description,
 the requirements for installation and so on.
 The source-, specification- and resulting RPM-files are organized in a
 specific directory structure which can be built automatically using a helper
 program 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rpm-full-howto-build-a-package"

\end_inset

.
 This step is desribed in more detail later in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Creating-the-required-dir-structure-RPM"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nevertheless, compared to the Debian system, RPM is more flexible (and therefore
 much more complex) regarding the features of its spec file.
 In contrast to a Debian package, a RPM package is normally freshly compiled
 from its sources.
 Therefore, the package build process consists of serveral steps, or as
 RPM calls it, stages, that are executed one after another.
 Every stage is designed to read files from one directory, to carry out
 some operations on these files and to write them back to the same or another
 directory.
 Therefore, the system uses some predefined variables, which all start with
 a percent sign (%), to address these stages and directories.
 The available stages and their functions are described in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Creating-the-specifications-file-RPM"

\end_inset

 in further detail.
 The following chapter will lead through the creation of such a RPM package.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Creating the RPM Package for Red Hat-based Linux Distributions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Creating-the-RPM"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To create the desired RPM package, a number of tasks has to be carried out.
 This includes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
installing the required programs, which is described in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
