\begin_layout Standard
Most of the file's settings are quite self-explanatory.
 
\family typewriter
Name
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
Version
\family default
, of course, define the resulting package's name and version.
 The version for BSF4ooRexx is defined in the file 
\family typewriter
BSF.CLS
\family default
 and is 406 at the time of writing this document.
 
\family typewriter
Release
\family default
, in this case, indicates the date of the distribution's current release.
 Here it was 20110822 which stands for August, 22nd 2011.
 
\family typewriter
Summary
\family default
 defines a short summary of the packages contents.
 The next setting, named 
\family typewriter
Group
\family default
, is very important for the package management system.
 It defines to which group of software this package belongs.
 We decided to use Development/Tools, because BSF4ooRexx can be seen as
 extension to ooRexx.
 
\family typewriter
License
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
URL
\family default
 define, under which license the package and its content can be used and
 where it can be found on the internet.
 A very useful setting is 
\family typewriter
BuildRoot
\family default
.
 It defines the name used for the build-subdirectory in the 
\family typewriter
BUILDROOT
\family default
 folder.
 Using placeholder variables for name (
\family typewriter
%{name}
\family default
), version (
\family typewriter
%{version}
\family default
) and release (
\family typewriter
%{release}
\family default
) it allows to build multiple versions of the package, whithout overwriting
 already existing older releases or versions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next block of settings has to do with requirements.

\family typewriter
 Autoreq
\family default
 defines, if the package management system should automatically try to determine
 required additional software during the package's installation.
 This can be a very useful feature, but in our case, we exactly know which
 requirements must be met and, therefore, this feature has been turned off,
 as the 0 (zero) indicates
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
During development, the automatic determination of additional software led
 to a large number of additional packages that had to be installed.
 Adding the 
\family typewriter
Autoreq
\family default
 parameter solved this issue.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The setting 
\family typewriter
BuildRequires
\family default
 allows to define packages that must be installed, in order for the build
 to succeed.
 The programs (packages) listed here, are used during the stages of the
 build process e.g.
 for compiling the source files.
 We don't want to compile any files.
 The only thing we do is unpack the BSF4ooRexx distribution file.
 Therefore, the only package listed here is 
\family typewriter
unzip
\family default
.
 In contrast to the build process requirements, 
\family typewriter
Requires
\family default
 lists packages that have to be present, to execute the files within this
 package.
 Because BSF4ooRexx needs Java
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For running BSF4ooRexx the Java Runtime Environment (JRE) is required.
 Nevertheless, there is no explicit package for the JRE availabe.
 It is included in the JDK package defined here.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and ooRexx to work properly, both have been added here with their required
 version.
 As one can see, the 
\family typewriter
Requires
\family default
 keyword can be used multiple time for defining a larger number of required
 programs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family typewriter
%description
\family default
 is a macro that allows the user to define a longer description for the
 package.
 In contrast to the 
\family typewriter
Summary
\family default
 tag, it can be used as a place for important information the user should
 see before the package is installed.
 
\end_layout

