\begin_layout Standard
For the creation of the installer package for Mac OS X, we used a program
 called Iceberg 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "iceberg-homepage"

\end_inset

.
 Beside the facts that it is free and easy to use, it fulfills our requirements
 for flexibility of configuration.
 It allows to define different types of scripts (as shown in chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Scripts-OSX"

\end_inset

) as well as the software requirements (e.g.
 that Java is installed in the correct version) that have to be met before
 the installation process is started.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

There are also alternatives to Iceberg e.g.
 PackageMaker that comes with XCode, Apple's Integrated Development Environment
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "xcode-homepage"

\end_inset

.
 The disadvantages of these alternatives is, that they are either not free
 or are lacking important features, such as the possibility of defining
 postflight scripts that are executed after installation.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Preliminary-Works-OSX"

\end_inset

 will describe necessary preliminary works, chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:The-Installer-OSX"

\end_inset

 will take a closer look on the features of Iceberg, followed by chapter
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Creating-the-Install-Package-OSX"

\end_inset

, which shows how an installer will be created.
 Last but not least, chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Use-Cases-OSX"

\end_inset

 will show the installer in action.
\end_layout

